 "topicId": "BTO-301",
 "slug": "why-qr-codes-have-three-corner-squares",
 "version": 1,
 "author": "visual-director skill (Claude Code session, hand-directed)",
 "treatment": {
  "educationalObjective": "The viewer leaves knowing that the three corner squares are position detection patterns, that their nested dark-light-dark structure produces a 1:1:3:1:1 run of black and white along any line through the centre, that this ratio was chosen because it is rare in print, and that three found corners (with one empty) tell a reader the code's orientation.",
  "visualHook": "Frame 0 is a real, scannable QR code. Within half a second every data module drops away and only the three squares remain, with a dashed question mark where a fourth corner would be: the viewer sees that the squares are a separate system before the narration says so.",
  "throughLine": "One real symbol is followed through the whole Short (hook, scan, anatomy, ratio, payoff); the empty fourth corner planted in the hook is resolved in the payoff.",
  "mechanismsToAnimate": [
   "data modules vs finder patterns",
   "a reader finding the three patterns",
   "the nested-square structure of one pattern",
   "the run-length ratio along a scan line",
   "invariance of that ratio when the code is rotated",
   "orientation from three corners plus one empty corner"
  ],
  "factualRisks": [
   "Finder layer sizes are drawn from the real encoded symbol (segno), not from narration; no layer dimensions are written on screen.",
   "The 1:1:3:1:1 runs are shown only along a line through the pattern's centre, where the ratio holds exactly; the rotation demo uses exact geometry (every crossing scales by 1/max(|cos|,|sin|)).",
   "The printed-matter samples and the business-form search are conceptual depictions of the documented survey and reasoning (CONCEPTUAL chip); no survey data or frequencies are shown.",
   "The code shown encodes 'HIDDEN LOGIC' (no URL) and is decoded in QA."
  ]
 },
